DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddrrbbrr functions provide an API to network drivers for using
     buf_ring(9) for enqueueing and dequeueing packets.  This is meant as a
     replacement for the IFQ interface for packet queuing.  It allows a packet
     to be enqueued with a single atomic and packet dequeue to be done without
     any per-packet atomics as it is protected by the driver's tx queue lock.
     If INVARIANTS is enabled, ddrrbbrr__ddeeqquueeuuee() will assert that the tx queue
     lock is held when it is called.

     The ddrrbbrr__ffrreeee() function frees all the enqueued mbufs and then frees the
     buf_ring.

     The ddrrbbrr__eennqquueeuuee() function is used to enqueue an mbuf to a buf_ring,
     falling back to the ifnet's IFQ if ALTQ(4) is enabled.

     The ddrrbbrr__ddeeqquueeuuee() function is used to dequeue an mbuf from a buf_ring
     or, if ALTQ(4) is enabled, from the ifnet's IFQ.

     The ddrrbbrr__ddeeqquueeuuee__ccoonndd() function is used to conditionally dequeue an mbuf
     from a buf_ring based on whether _f_u_n_c returns TRUE or FALSE.

     The ddrrbbrr__fflluusshh() function frees all mbufs enqueued in the buf_ring and
     the ifnet's IFQ.

     The ddrrbbrr__eemmppttyy() function returns TRUE if there are no mbufs enqueued,
     FALSE otherwise.

     The ddrrbbrr__iinnuussee() function returns the number of mbufs enqueued.  Note to
     users that this is intrinsically racy as there is no guarantee that there
     will not be more mbufs when ddrrbbrr__ddeeqquueeuuee() is actually called.  Provided
     the tx queue lock is held there will not be less.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ddrrbbrr__eennqquueeuuee() function returns ENOBUFS if there are no slots avail‐
     able in the buf_ring and 0 on success.

     The ddrrbbrr__ddeeqquueeuuee() and ddrrbbrr__ddeeqquueeuuee__ccoonndd() functions return an mbuf on
     success and NULL if the buf_ring is empty.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     These functions were introduced in FreeBSD 8.0.

